quick review before i put this up. mainly worried about the retry logic, i wrote it late

```ts
export type RetryOptions = {
  attempts: number;
  baseDelayMs: number;
  maxDelayMs: number;
  isRetryable: (error: unknown) => boolean;
};

const DEFAULTS: RetryOptions = {
  attempts: 3,
  baseDelayMs: 200,
  maxDelayMs: 5_000,
  isRetryable: () => true,
};

function delayFor(attempt: number, options: RetryOptions): number {
  const exponential = options.baseDelayMs * 2 ** attempt;
  const capped = Math.min(exponential, options.maxDelayMs);
  return capped / 2 + Math.random() * (capped / 2);
}

export async function withRetry<T>(
  operation: () => Promise<T>,
  overrides: Partial<RetryOptions> = {},
): Promise<T> {
  const options = { ...DEFAULTS, ...overrides };
  let lastError: unknown;
  for (let attempt = 0; attempt < options.attempts; attempt += 1) {
    try {
      return await operation();
    } catch (error) {
      lastError = error;
      if (!options.isRetryable(error)) throw error;
      await sleep(delayFor(attempt, options));
    }
  }
  throw lastError;
}
```

things i already know are wrong: no abort signal, and sleeping after the last attempt is wasteful. what i actually want to know is whether the jitter is doing what i think it's doing and whether throwing lastError loses the stack. also is `attempts: 3` three total or three retries plus the first call, i keep flip flopping on which one callers expect and i've seen both conventions in libraries we use.
